<spell-notebook "date usage">
	<header level="1">
		Date
		<header>A date says when something happened, inline and small inside a feed event.</>
	</>
	<date-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<html>
			<ui-menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
				<ui-item link="#using-it">Using it</>
				<ui-item link="#owners">Owners</>
				<ui-item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			`<ui-date>` says when something happened: a feed event's date, inline and small inside its summary or above it.

			- `datetime` makes it a real `<time datetime>`, so machines read the date too: an ISO date
			  (`2026-10-04`), a time, or a duration (`P3D`).
			- The text is what readers see: "3 days ago", "Today".
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Owners">
		<markdown>
			`<ui-date>` finds its nearest owner and sets `:state(in-<owner>)` on itself; `UIParts.css` styles it by that
			state. Its owners, and what each makes of it:

			- [Feed](/components/ui/ui-feed.html) (`:state(in-feed)`): small and muted:  above the summary, or inline at its end

			Anywhere else it is an unstyled block. More on how parts find their owners: [Content parts](/components/ui/ui-header.html).
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- With `datetime` it renders a `<time datetime>`: the visible text ("3 days ago") is read aloud, the exact date is
			  there for machines.
			- No role or tab stop of its own.
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
